<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Guinea-Bissau</h1><p class="st-meta">1973 CE – present · capital Bissau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small West African republic that won independence from Portugal in 1973 after a war led by the PAIGC, and has since had repeated military coups.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paigc">PAIGC</a>, founded in 1956 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amilcar-cabral">Amílcar Cabral</a>, launched the Guinea-Bissau War of Independence on 23 January 1963 against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-guinea">Portuguese Guinea</a>. Independence was declared unilaterally on 24 September 1973 and recognised by Portugal on 10 September 1974, with Luís Cabral as first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paigc">PAIGC</a> ruled as a one-party socialist state. On 14 November 1980 João Bernardo Vieira overthrew Luís Cabral, and a Military Council of the Revolution governed until 1984. The first multi-party elections were held in 1994. An army uprising in May 1998 began a civil war and led to the president's ousting in June 1999, and Kumba Ialá was elected in 2000. Further coups followed in 2003 and 2012, and Vieira was assassinated on 2 March 2009. Until José Mário Vaz in 2019, no president completed a full five-year term.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Umaro Sissoco Embaló took office in February 2020 and faced attempted coups in 2022 and 2023. On 26 November 2025, three days after a general election, soldiers announced on state television that they had seized power. General Horta Inta-A Na Man has since served as transitional president, and the country's membership of ECOWAS and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> was suspended.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/guinea-bissau.jpg" alt="Guinea-Bissau" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Guinea-Bissau.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1973 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bissau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Luís Cabral, João Bernardo Vieira, Kumba Ialá, José Mário Vaz, Umaro Sissoco Embaló</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, Christianity, traditional faiths</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Guinea-Bissau Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Guinea-Bissau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=guinea-bissau" aria-label="Guinea-Bissau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Guinea-Bissau lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g33000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6194 3993L6194 3993"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6194 3993h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6194 3993h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6194 3993h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bissau. <a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=guinea-bissau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
